      "content": "I got a bit carried away with the goblin analogy. Let me try that again, with a bit more focus on the technical aspects.\n\nThe pitot tube works by measuring the pressure difference between the air flowing into the tube (the pitot static port) and the air flowing out of the tube (the static port). The pressure difference is directly proportional to the airspeed of the aircraft. The pitot tube is typically connected to a pitot-static system, which includes a pitot tube, a static port, and a pressure sensor.\n\nThe pitot tube is essentially a tube that draws in air from the surrounding environment, and it's connected to a pressure sensor that measures the air pressure difference between the front and rear of the tube. The pressure sensor is usually a static pressure sensor, which measures the static pressure of the air in the surrounding environment.\n\nThe pressure difference between the pitot and static ports is calculated using the Bernoulli's principle, which states that the pressure of a fluid (in this case, air) decreases as its velocity increases. By measuring the pressure difference between the pitot and static ports, the pressure sensor can calculate the airspeed of the aircraft.\n\nThe airspeed is then calculated using the following formula:\n\nV",
